<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">statuses</ansi>

A critical hit does more than hurt. The blow leaves a mark that matches the
weapon, and the mark is a status: a timed effect on whoever was struck. Enemies
suffer them too, and so do you. A status that costs damage or an action is
told to the room as it happens.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What a critical hit leaves</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">slashing</ansi>, claw weapons  bleeding
  <ansi fg="command">stabbing</ansi>            deep bleeding: two stacks at once
  <ansi fg="command">bludgeoning</ansi>         staggered
  <ansi fg="command">cleaving</ansi>            knocked down (40%), armor broken (40%), stunned (20%)
  <ansi fg="command">shooting</ansi>            exposed
  <ansi fg="command">whipping</ansi>            hobbled

A weapon with its own critical effect uses that one instead, and that one
can land even when the armor stops the blow; otherwise only a critical hit
that gets through the armor leaves a mark. Fists and a beast's natural
attacks leave none, and spells never critically hit. The mark is named in the hit's brackets:
<ansi fg="command">(critical hit, 6 damage, bleeding)</ansi>. On you or a companion, a critical hit that
gets through also leaves a wound, from any weapon, fists and bites too:
<ansi fg="command">(critical hit, 6 damage, bleeding, wounded)</ansi> (<ansi fg="command">help wounds</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The statuses</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">Bleeding</ansi>      1 damage each combat round for each stack, for 3 rounds. It
                  stacks up to 3, and each new cut renews the time.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Staggered</ansi>     loses its next action.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Knocked down</ansi>  loses its next action, and is easier to hit while it is
                  down (-4 speed) for 1 more round: 2 rounds in all.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Stunned</ansi>       loses its next two actions, and can't dodge or block with a
                  shield while stunned (its armor still counts): 2 rounds.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Armor broken</ansi>  its armor gives half its protection for the rest of the
                  fight.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Exposed</ansi>       the chance of a critical hit against it rises by 25 points
                  (from 10% to 35%, say), for 2 rounds.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Hobbled</ansi>       cannot <ansi fg="command">flee</ansi>, even a flight already begun, and moves
                  slowly (-3 speed), for 3 rounds.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Burning</ansi>       2 fire damage each combat round, for 4 rounds. Water puts
                  it out.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Overloaded</ansi>    casts poorly (-30 to its chance to cast) and moves slowly
                  (-3 speed), for 3 rounds. Shower of Sparks overloads what
                  it sears.

A fighter who loses its action does nothing that round: no blow, no spell
round, no flight. It is told so in the line, as in
<ansi fg="command">The bandit captain reels, and loses the action. (staggered)</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">How long they last</ansi>

Statuses count combat rounds (8 seconds each), not the game's 4-second
rounds, and they all end when the fight does. A blow struck in a fight's
last round leaves no status: the fight is already over. A status that took damage
reads <ansi fg="command">The first cutthroat bleeds. (1 damage, bleeding)</ansi>, and its end reads
<ansi fg="command">The first cutthroat's bleeding stops.</ansi>. Damage from a status can bring
someone down like any other. A bleed that runs its full course on you or
a companion leaves a light wound, which closes when the fight ends. See
what ails you with <ansi fg="command">conditions</ansi>.

The battle summary counts the damage they do (<ansi fg="command">help battle-summary</ansi>), and
the rest of a fight is explained in <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi> and
<ansi fg="command">help narration</ansi>.
